Current State Assessment
4M transactions/month = ~1.5/sec average
But payments are never uniform. Realistically:

Peak hours could be 10-50x average = 15-75/sec
Each payment touches 5 function apps + ~15 Cosmos operations + 2 Service Bus messages


Areas to Address
1. Azure Functions Scaling
By default isolated worker functions scale automatically but you need the right hosting plan.
Current risk: Consumption plan has cold start delays and scale limits.
Fix: Use Premium Plan (EP1 or EP2) for all 5 function apps:

Always-warm instances — no cold starts
Pre-warmed instances handle burst traffic
Set minimum instance count to 2-3 per service

Gateway          → EP1, min 2 instances
AccountResolution → EP1, min 2 instances
Compliance        → EP2, min 2 instances (Alloy calls are slow)
Transfer          → EP1, min 2 instances
EventNotification → EP1, min 2 instances

2. Service Bus — Premium Tier
Your current Service Bus tier matters significantly.
Standard tier limitations:

Max message size 256KB
No guaranteed throughput
Shared infrastructure

Fix: Upgrade to Premium tier:

Dedicated messaging units
1MB message size
Predictable latency
Set 1 messaging unit minimum, scale to 2-4 at peak

Also tune your subscriptions:
json"prefetchCount": 20,        // currently 10 — increase for throughput
"maxConcurrentCalls": 16,   // already set correctly

3. Cosmos DB — Request Units
Each payment does approximately:

Gateway: 2 writes (idempotency + transaction) = ~20 RUs
AccountResolution: 4-5 reads = ~25 RUs
Compliance: 2 reads/writes = ~10 RUs
Transfer: 6 writes (2 entries + 2 balance patches + 2 reads) = ~60 RUs
EventNotification: 1 write = ~10 RUs

Total: ~125 RUs per payment
At 75/sec peak: 9,375 RUs/sec needed
Fix: Set Cosmos containers to autoscale:
tchSendTransactions  → autoscale max 10,000 RU/s
tchSendIdempotency   → autoscale max 5,000 RU/s
ledgers              → autoscale max 10,000 RU/s
ledgerEntries        → autoscale max 10,000 RU/s

4. Idempotency — Gateway Bottleneck
Currently the Gateway does two sequential Cosmos writes before publishing to Service Bus. At high volume this is the first bottleneck.
Fix: Make the idempotency check a conditional upsert instead of read-then-write:
csharp// Instead of read then create
// Use CreateItemAsync with conflict detection
try
{
    await _container.CreateItemAsync(idempotencyRecord, ...);
}
catch (CosmosException ex) when (ex.StatusCode == HttpStatusCode.Conflict)
{
    // Duplicate — return 409
    return isDuplicate = true;
}
This saves one Cosmos round trip per payment.

5. AccountResolution — Caching
At 4M/month, the same accounts will be resolved repeatedly. Currently every payment does 3 Cosmos reads for account resolution.
Fix: Add Azure Cache for Redis:
csharp// Cache resolved account for 24 hours
var cacheKey = $"account:{accountNumber}";
var cached = await _cache.GetAsync<AccountResolutionResult>(cacheKey);
if (cached is not null) return cached;

// Resolve from Cosmos
var result = await ResolveFromCosmosAsync(accountNumber);

// Cache result
await _cache.SetAsync(cacheKey, result, TimeSpan.FromHours(24));
return result;
This could reduce AccountResolution Cosmos reads by 80-90%.

6. Transfer — Prefund Resolution Caching
Currently every payment does 3 Cosmos reads to resolve the RTPPrefund account. This is the same data every time for a given fintechId.
Fix: Cache the PrefundResolutionResult in memory since it almost never changes:
csharp// In-memory cache — singleton lifetime
private static readonly ConcurrentDictionary<string, PrefundResolutionResult> 
    _prefundCache = new();

public async Task<PrefundResolutionResult?> ResolvePrefundLedgerAsync(string fintechId, ...)
{
    if (_prefundCache.TryGetValue(fintechId, out var cached))
        return cached;

    var result = await ResolveFromCosmosAsync(fintechId, ...);
    if (result is not null)
        _prefundCache[fintechId] = result;

    return result;
}

7. Compliance — Alloy SLA
Once Alloy is integrated, KYC and TMS calls will be the slowest part of the pipeline. If Alloy takes 2-3 seconds per call and you're running source + destination in parallel, that's still 2-3 seconds per payment in Compliance.
Mitigation:

Cache KYC results by entityId with a 31-day TTL (you already had this logic in the Node code)
This means returning customers skip the Alloy call entirely


8. Observability — Critical at Scale
At 4M/month you cannot debug issues manually. You need:
Application Insights:

Correlate all logs by evolveId and correlationId across all 5 services
Set up alerts on dead letter queue depth
Alert on function error rate > 1%

Service Bus Dead Letter Monitoring:

Each subscription has a dead letter queue
Set up an alert when DLQ depth > 10

Cosmos Monitoring:

Alert on RU consumption > 80% of provisioned
Alert on throttled requests (429s)


9. Load Testing — Before Go-Live
Before handling real traffic, run a load test:
Tool: Azure Load Testing or k6
Target: Gateway POST /tptch/send
Ramp: 0 → 75 req/sec over 5 minutes
Duration: 30 minutes at peak
Watch for:

Service Bus message lag growing
Cosmos 429 throttling
Function cold starts
Dead letter queue accumulation


